1. Random Sampling Results

A random sample of 25 patients produced a mean Glucose close to the population mean, showing small samples can still estimate averages well.

The maximum Glucose in the sample differed more, because extreme values vary a lot in small samples.

2. BMI Percentile Results

The 98th percentile of BMI from the sample was close to the population percentile but showed more variation.

High percentiles (95+, 98+) are sensitive and need larger samples for stability.

3. Bootstrap Findings (500 samples, n=150)

Bootstrap mean of BloodPressure was almost identical to the population mean.

Bootstrap standard deviation matched the population variation closely.

Bootstrap 98th percentile had small fluctuations but stayed near the true percentile.

4. Distribution Shape

The bootstrap mean distribution looked approximately normal, supporting the Central Limit Theorem.
